<title>StringWriter.writeEncodedLine Function</title>

<i>This documentation also applies to the <logiccolor>Report.writeEncodedLine</logiccolor> function as a <logiccolor>Report</logiccolor> is a type of <logiccolor>StringWriter</logiccolor>.</i>


<subheader>Format 1</subheader>

<logicsyntax>
b = object_name.writeEncodedLine(<arg>string_exp</arg>『, <arg>argument1</arg>, ..., <arg>argumentN</arg>』)
</logicsyntax>


<subheader>Format 2</subheader>

<logicsyntax>
b = object_name.writeEncodedLine(<arg>msg_num</arg>『, <arg>argument1</arg>, ..., <arg>argumentN</arg>』)
</logicsyntax>


<subheader>Description</subheader>

The <logiccolor>StringWriter.writeEncodedLine</logiccolor> function writes text to a <link StringWriter.csdoc>StringWriter</link> object's string buffer (or to a <link templated_reports.csdoc>templated report</link>), followed by the <link newline_handling.csdoc>newline</link> character <logiccolor>'\n'</logiccolor>. The text is written after <link encode_function.csdoc>encoding</link> the text, and the newline character, for the StringWriter's <link StringWriter_statement.csdoc>encoding type</link> or the templated report's <link change_report_properties.csdoc>format</link>. When used as part of a <logiccolor>Report</logiccolor> object, the function is meant to be called from within the templated report's logic.

<include include_StringWriter_write.hgi />


<subheader>Example 2 (Report)</subheader>

<report HTML>
<?
    HOUSEHOLD_REPORT.writeEncodedLine("The report was generated at: ", timestamp());

    // alternatively, use $ to refer to HOUSEHOLD_REPORT
    $.writeEncodedLine("The report was generated at: ", timestamp());
?>
</report>


<seealso StringWriter.csdoc templated_reports.csdoc />
